{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('state.json'))\nwant={'ba97eea3cb1c45009b75f70720a38dfd-interpretation-0':'transfer','d33dfeeee4124704bae38664b5b63da2-interpretation-0':'shutdown'}\nfor i in st['interpretations']:\n    if i.get('id') in want:\n        print('=====',want[i['id']],i['id'])\n        print('COUNTER:',json.dumps(i['counterevidence'],indent=1)[:1200])\n        print('LIMITS:',json.dumps(i['limitations'],indent=1)[:1500])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"===== transfer ba97eea3cb1c45009b75f70720a38dfd-interpretation-0\nCOUNTER: [\n \"The API comment does not state that the target must be a voter, and a caller who names only voters never reaches this path, so the defect depends on an accepted calling pattern rather than on the library's own choice of target.\",\n \"LeadershipTransfer() without an explicit target filters non-voters in pickServer, which shows the intended transfer target set is the voter set and supports reading the targeted call as requiring the same premise.\",\n \"A TimeoutNow that cannot be delivered (unreachable target) is reported as an error by the transfer path, so not every unusable target is silently accepted.\",\n \"The interruption is bounded: the remaining voters re-elect after their election timers, and the control case shows the probe does observe a completed transfer, so the outcome is a wrong success report and a leadership interruption rather than a cluster that never recovers.\"\n]\nLIMITS: [\n \"The reported-result and interval observations rest on two executions of this probe (receipts b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd and 23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0); across them the leader holding office at the end differed (in the first, the previous leader was Leader again at term 4; in the second, another voter), so the identity of the successor is timing dependent and is recorded rather than asserted.\",\n \"The leaderless interval is an observed span at 1ms sampling resolution between a 200ms election timeout and a single-process in-memory transport; it is not a bound for configurations with different timeouts, and it does not distinguish time spent campaigning from time waiting for election timers.\",\n \"The control case with a voter target finished in about one millisecond, so its sampling window is too short for its zero leaderless count to say anything about longer gaps.\",\n \"The claim concerns the reported result of the call, the requested target's inability to win and the observed interruption; it does not assert data loss or that the cluster fails to recover, since a voter is elected afterwards.\",\n \"The probe uses one process with real Raft instances and in-memory transports, so it exercises the leader's own transfer, election and vote paths but not process-level partitions or multi-host clocks.\"\n]\n===== shutdown d33dfeeee4124704bae38664b5b63da2-interpretation-0\nCOUNTER: [\n \"The racing form did not reproduce in the later revisions (0 of 128 calls per case in two runs), so the earlier 9 and 41 are a timing-dependent manifestation rather than a rate.\",\n \"Without BatchApplyCh the apply path is never affected: Barrier and Apply answered every post-shutdown call in all three runs (indices 3, 9, 15 and 5, 11, 17 here), so the defect reaches the apply path only under a configuration whose own comment already warns that it breaks the Apply timeout guarantee.\",\n \"Roughly half of the calls in each affected case are answered with the documented shutdown error rather than stranded, so the outcome is a per-call select choice, and no call that was answered carried a wrong outcome.\",\n \"The repository's test file records the same class as a known TODO, which is evidence about intent, not a reason to treat an unbounded block as acceptable.\",\n \"Over-subscribing verifyCh five-fold did not remove the strand: the 320-call cases stranded 2, 3 and 2 of eight post-shutdown calls, against 3, 7 and 5 of eight for the 128-call cases, so the outcome is not decided by spare capacity being available when the loop exits.\"\n]\nLIMITS: [\n \"The post-shutdown counts per case come from three trials of eight calls each (VerifyLeader 11 of 24 stranded; batched Barrier 11 of 24; batched Apply 13 of 24), so they bound the mechanism rather than a population rate.\",\n \"The strand is classified by a 500ms window; permanence is inferred from the source, since no reader of the affected channel remains and these futures carry no shutdown channel.\",\n \"Single-voter node, in-memory transport, 50ms timeouts, one process: no peer round trip or networked shutdown is involved.\",\n \"The over-subscribed cases carry 320 concurrent racing calls, five times the 64 verifyCh slots, so a large share of sends could not be accepted; the buffer still ended with free slots and the strand persisted, which means occupancy at the exit is not what the probe controls.\"\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
